{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update-stage8.json\", \"summary\": \"New A2/A1 boundary from the recovery work, recorded honestly as an open concern rather than a defect (valid:true).\\n\\nSourced behaviour promoted: InstallSnapshot checks freshness only by term (raft.go:1825-1845). On success it restores the FSM from the requested snapshot, then sets lastApplied to the request's LastLogIndex, sets the last snapshot index from the request, replaces both configurations from the request, and compacts or removes old logs by the request's index (raft.go:1926-1955). There is no comparison of the request's LastLogIndex with the receiver's own lastApplied, last snapshot index or log store range, so a request that is current in term but older in index has no receiving-side defence. Two supporting behaviours are recorded as well: recovery from a restored index depends on processLogs walking from lastApplied+1 to the next commit index out of the log and panicking when an entry is missing (raft.go:1292-1348), and the sender only opens a snapshot when it cannot produce entries from its per-peer nextIndex, which moves solely on follower responses via max(min(nextIndex-1, LastLog+1), 1) (replication.go:224-296, 296-380).\\n\\nQuestion registered as paused with a discriminator and resume conditions (@install-snapshot-index-rewind): can a delivered InstallSnapshot whose index is below the receiving node's applied index rewind acknowledged applied state, and is the rewind always covered by re-application from the surviving log? The interpretation is explicitly unresolved: the handler's lack of an index check is established, reachability is not. The strongest candidate protection recorded as counterevidence is sender-side — a follower reports LastLog = max(lastLogIndex, lastSnapshotIndex), so the backoff clamps nextIndex towards that snapshot-aware value rather than below it, which makes the decrement path stop at a matching prefix — but I did not prove it, so the decisive observation (a delivered request with LastLogIndex below the receiver's lastApplied while the receiver's log first index is above that value) is left for a harness rather than asserted.\\n\\nNo new defect claim, no new knowledge correction, and knowledge entry 87206f99921a4f25986b0b6525d9d58c-knowledge-0 is used only for what it establishes: the observed relation between a surviving log store index and an installed snapshot index. Validation of the returned bytes returns valid:true.\"}"}}
